      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm not quite sure I follow the comparison there. Can we stick to the legal specifics? If a tenant experiences a prolonged business interruption due to government-mandated lockdowns—which aren't covered by \"physical damage\"—how do Singaporean courts typically determine if that qualifies under the force majeure clause?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the detour. In Singapore, courts typically consider the following factors when determining whether a government-mandated lockdown qualifies as a force majeure event:\n\nFirstly, the courts examine whether the lockdown was unforeseen at the time of the lease agreement. If the tenant had knowledge of the possibility of a lockdown, it may not be considered a force majeure event.\n\nSecondly, the courts consider whether the lockdown was beyond the control of the tenant. If the tenant could have taken reasonable steps to mitigate the impact of the lockdown, such as by diversifying their business or having a contingency plan, it may not be considered a force majeure event.\n\nThirdly, the courts assess whether the lockdown had a significant impact on the tenant's ability to perform their lease obligations. If the lockdown resulted in a significant reduction in business activity, it may be considered a force majeure event.\n\nIn the case of a prolonged business interruption, the courts may also consider whether the tenant has taken reasonable steps to mitigate the impact of the interruption, such as by seeking alternative business arrangements or by negotiating with the landlord.\n\nIt's worth noting that the courts have considered cases where government-mandated lockdowns have been deemed a force maje",
