      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. To follow up on the mitigation aspect, if a tenant argues that the interruption was an \"act of God\" or an unpredictable disruption that they couldn't have mitigated, how do the courts distinguish between a standard commercial risk and an actual force majeure event?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In Singapore, the courts have established that an \"act of God\" or an unpredictable disruption that is beyond the control of the parties is more likely to be considered a force majeure event if it is shown to be unforeseen and unavoidable.\n\nTo distinguish between a standard commercial risk and an actual force majeure event, the courts consider whether the tenant had reasonable knowledge of the possibility of the disruption occurring. If the tenant had knowledge of the possibility of a disruption, it may be considered a commercial risk rather than a force majeure event.\n\nFor example, if a tenant has a business continuity plan in place to mitigate the impact of a cyber-attack, and the cyber-attack occurs, the courts may consider whether the tenant's plan was adequate and whether they took reasonable steps to mitigate the impact of the attack. If the tenant had not taken adequate steps to mitigate the impact of the attack, it may be considered a commercial risk rather than a force majeure event.\n\nOn the other hand, if the tenant had no knowledge of the possibility of a disruption, and the disruption was unforeseen and unavoidable, it may be considered a force majeure event. The courts will consider whether the tenant had taken reasonable steps to mitigate the impact",
